            <ArticleTitle>The pristine graphene produced by liquid exfoliation of graphite in mixed solvent and its application to determination of dopamine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>279-286</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0021-9797(17)31341-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jcis.2017.11.038</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The pristine graphene can be easily prepared in isopropanol-water mixture with salts as assistant via liquid-phase exfoliation method. The concentration of graphene dispersion reaches as high as 0.565 mg/mL. The graphene film prepared by drop-casting method shows an excellent electrical conductivity (7.095 × 104 S/m). Furthermore, an electrochemical biosensor based on the pristine graphene shows high selectivity and sensitivity for the determination of dopamine. The linear detection range for dopamine is 2.5-1500 μM with detection limit of 1.5 μM. This method provides a potential process for preparing high-quality graphene ready-to-use in low-boiling point solvent.</AbstractText>
